ivities.",\n        domain: "Classroom Management",\n        difficulty: 1,\n        pointValue: 5\n      },\n      {\n        id: 12,\n        question: "What is the most appropriate way to display children\'s artwork in a preschool classroom?",\n        options: [\n          "Only display the best pieces that meet teacher standards",\n          "Post all artwork at children\'s eye level with their permission",\n          "Have teachers correct mistakes before displaying work",\n          "Only display artwork of children who follow directions well"\n        ],\n        correctAnswer: "Post all artwork at children\'s eye level with their permission",\n        explanation: "Displaying artwork at children\'s eye level shows respect for their perspective, while getting permission honors their agency and ownership of their creations.",\n        domain: "Learning Environment",\n        difficulty: 1,\n        pointValue: 5\n      }\n    ];\n\n    const level2Questions = [\n      {\n        id: 4,\n        question: "What is the main purpose of the CLASS assessment tool in early childhood settings?",\n        options: [\n          "To evaluate physical facilities only",\n          "To assess teacher-child interactions",\n          "To measure children\'s academic achievement",\n          "To ensure compliance with licensing requirements"\n        ],\n        correctAnswer: "To assess teacher-child interactions",\n        explanation: "The Classroom Assessment Scoring System (CLASS) measures the quality of teacher-child interactions.",\n        domain: "Assessment",\n        difficulty: 2,\n        pointValue: 10\n      },\n      {\n        id: 5,\n        question: "Which approach best represents authentic assessment in early childhood?",\n        options: [\n          "Weekly standardized tests",\n          "Observation during natural play",\n          "Workbooks completed at home",\n          "Comparing children to age norms only"\n        ],\n        correctAnswer: "Observation during natural play",\n        explanation: "Authentic assessment gathers information about children\'s abilities during regular activities and play.",\n        domain: "Assessment",\n        difficulty: 2,\n        pointValue: 10\n      },\n      {\n        id: 6,\n        question: "According to Vygotsky\'s Zone of Proximal Development, when should teachers provide scaffolding?",\n        options: [\n          "Only when children ask for help",\n          "For tasks children can already do independently",\n          "For tasks slightly beyond current ability",\n          "Only during formal instruction"\n        ],\n        correctAnswer: "For tasks slightly beyond current ability",\n        explanation: "Scaffolding is most effective in the zone between what a child can do independently and what they cannot do yet.",\n        domain: "Child Development",\n        difficulty: 2,\n        pointValue: 10\n      },\n      {\n        id: 13,\n        question: "When using \'serve and return\' interactions with infants and toddlers, what are you primarily supporting?",\n        options: [\n          "Table manners for meal times",\n          "Their need for constant entertainment",\n          "Brain architecture and secure attachments",\n          "Future athletic abilities"\n        ],\n        correctAnswer: "Brain architecture and secure attachments",\n        explanation: "Serve and return interactions, where adults respond appropriately to a child\'s communication attempts, strengthen neural connections and build secure relationships that are foundational for development.",\n        domain: "Brain Development",\n        difficulty: 2,\n        pointValue: 10\n      },\n      {\n        id: 14,\n        question: "A child in your class frequently bites others when frustrated. Which approach is most developmentally appropriate?",\n        options: [\n          "Time-out until they apologize",\n          "Bite them back so they understand how it feels",\n 